@startuml fig-tcp-vs-udp-wireshark
skinparam backgroundColor white
skinparam defaultFontName Arial
skinparam defaultFontSize 11
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center

title TCP vs UDP — What Wireshark Reveals

box "TCP Session (port 12345)" #E8F5E9
  participant "TCP Client" as TC #FFE0B2
  participant "TCP Server" as TS #C8E6C9
end box

box "UDP Exchange (port 12345)" #FFF3E0
  participant "UDP Client" as UC #FFE0B2
  participant "UDP Server" as US #C8E6C9
end box

== TCP: CONNECTION SETUP (3 packets) ==
TC -> TS : SYN
TS --> TC : SYN-ACK
TC -> TS : ACK

== TCP: DATA (2+ packets) ==
TC -> TS : PSH-ACK "hello"
TS --> TC : PSH-ACK "HELLO"

== TCP: TEARDOWN (3 packets) ==
TC -> TS : FIN-ACK
TS --> TC : FIN-ACK
TC -> TS : ACK

== UDP: DATA (2 packets total) ==
UC -> US : "hello" (single datagram)
US --> UC : "HELLO" (single datagram)

legend bottom
  TCP packet count depends on ACK coalescing, segmentation and teardown.
  UDP: one request and one reply datagram in this successful example.
  TCP groups packets into a stream (tcp.stream).
  UDP preserves datagrams; Wireshark can assign udp.stream indices.
endlegend
@enduml
